Task completion (``client.complete_task``, used by both a Plone user
submitting a BPM task form and the ``completeEditTask``/``completeAddTask``
auto-complete subscribers) now retries a few times, with a short randomized
backoff, when Operaton reports its own optimistic-locking conflict -- the
race two parallel multi-instance branches (e.g. two reviewers submitting
within the same instant, see ``examples/review-process``) can hit on the
same execution's variable scope. Previously this surfaced straight back as
"Unexpected error on submit."

Every deferred BPM side effect dispatched through a ``SideEffectDataManager``
(BPM Signal/Message content rule actions, and the two auto-complete
subscribers) now also votes on the Plone transaction it is joined to: a
quick, unauthenticated ``GET .../engine`` check that the engine is reachable
before the transaction commits (``client.check_engine_reachable``).
Previously an unreachable engine was only discovered after Plone's own
state had already committed, in a background thread, with the failure only
logged -- Plone and Operaton silently drifted out of sync instead of the
whole action being refused up front. This check is memoized per
transaction, so joining more than one side effect to the same transaction
(e.g. an edit that both auto-completes a task and fires a content rule
action) still only probes the engine once. The new ``client.join_side_effect``
helper centralizes joining a ``SideEffectDataManager`` with this vote wired
in, so a future call site cannot forget it.

The shared background executor these deferred side effects run on
(``utils.SIDE_EFFECT_WORKER``) is no longer a single thread: with
``complete_task``'s retry backoff now sleeping in it, one task's
optimistic-locking retries would otherwise queue up every other unrelated
BPM side effect site-wide behind it.
